OBJECTIVE:

Understand how to resolve conflicts during a Git merge when
master and a branch contain different changes to the same file.


PREREQUISITE:

Complete the previous Git branching and merging lab.


--------------------------------------------------
1. CHECK MASTER STATUS
--------------------------------------------------

git checkout master

git status

Make sure the working directory is clean.


--------------------------------------------------
2. CREATE GitWork BRANCH
--------------------------------------------------

git branch GitWork

git checkout GitWork


--------------------------------------------------
3. CREATE hello.xml
--------------------------------------------------

touch hello.xml

Add some content:

echo "<hello>Branch Version</hello>" > hello.xml


--------------------------------------------------
4. CHECK STATUS
--------------------------------------------------

git status


--------------------------------------------------
5. COMMIT CHANGES TO GitWork
--------------------------------------------------

git add hello.xml

git commit -m "Added hello.xml in GitWork"


--------------------------------------------------
6. SWITCH TO MASTER
--------------------------------------------------

git checkout master


--------------------------------------------------
7. CREATE hello.xml IN MASTER
--------------------------------------------------

Create the same file with DIFFERENT content:

echo "<hello>Master Version</hello>" > hello.xml


--------------------------------------------------
8. COMMIT MASTER CHANGES
--------------------------------------------------

git add hello.xml

git commit -m "Added hello.xml in master"


--------------------------------------------------
9. VIEW COMPLETE LOG
--------------------------------------------------

git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all


This shows the different commits on master and GitWork.


--------------------------------------------------
10. CHECK DIFFERENCES
--------------------------------------------------

git diff master GitWork


--------------------------------------------------
11. USE P4MERGE
--------------------------------------------------

git difftool master GitWork

P4Merge displays the differences visually.


--------------------------------------------------
12. MERGE GitWork INTO MASTER
--------------------------------------------------

git checkout master

git merge GitWork


A conflict will occur because both master and GitWork
modified/created hello.xml differently.


--------------------------------------------------
13. CHECK CONFLICT
--------------------------------------------------

git status


Git will show hello.xml as a conflicted file.


--------------------------------------------------
14. OPEN hello.xml
--------------------------------------------------

The file will contain conflict markers similar to:

<<<<<<< HEAD
<hello>Master Version</hello>
=======
<hello>Branch Version</hello>
>>>>>>> GitWork


Meaning:

<<<<<<< HEAD
    → Changes from master

=======
    → Separator

>>>>>>> GitWork
    → Changes from GitWork


--------------------------------------------------
15. RESOLVE THE CONFLICT
--------------------------------------------------

Open hello.xml and decide which content should remain.

For example:

<hello>Final Version</hello>

IMPORTANT:
Remove all conflict markers:

<<<<<<< HEAD
=======
>>>>>>> GitWork


--------------------------------------------------
16. ADD THE RESOLVED FILE
--------------------------------------------------

git add hello.xml


--------------------------------------------------
17. COMMIT THE RESOLUTION
--------------------------------------------------

git commit -m "Resolved merge conflict"


--------------------------------------------------
18. CHECK STATUS
--------------------------------------------------

git status


The working directory should be clean.


--------------------------------------------------
19. IGNORE BACKUP FILE
--------------------------------------------------

If a backup file was created during conflict resolution,
add its pattern to .gitignore.

Example:

echo "*.orig" >> .gitignore


Check:

cat .gitignore


--------------------------------------------------
20. COMMIT .gitignore
--------------------------------------------------

git add .gitignore

git commit -m "Updated gitignore"


--------------------------------------------------
21. LIST ALL BRANCHES
--------------------------------------------------

git branch -a


--------------------------------------------------
22. DELETE MERGED BRANCH
--------------------------------------------------

git branch -d GitWork


--------------------------------------------------
23. VIEW FINAL LOG
--------------------------------------------------

git log --oneline --graph --decorate


--------------------------------------------------
COMPLETE FLOW
--------------------------------------------------

Check master
      ↓
Create GitWork
      ↓
Create hello.xml
      ↓
Commit branch changes
      ↓
Switch to master
      ↓
Create hello.xml with different content
      ↓
Commit master changes
      ↓
View log
      ↓
Check differences
      ↓
Use P4Merge
      ↓
Merge GitWork into master
      ↓
CONFLICT
      ↓
Open hello.xml
      ↓
Remove conflict markers
      ↓
Keep desired content
      ↓
git add hello.xml
      ↓
git commit
      ↓
Update .gitignore
      ↓
Commit .gitignore
      ↓
Delete GitWork
      ↓
View final log


--------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT COMMANDS
--------------------------------------------------

git status
    → Check current status

git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all
    → View complete branch history

git diff master GitWork
    → Show differences

git difftool master GitWork
    → Show visual differences using P4Merge

git merge GitWork
    → Merge branch into master

git add hello.xml
    → Mark conflict as resolved

git commit
    → Complete the merge

git branch -a
    → List all branches

git branch -d GitWork
    → Delete merged branch


KEY POINT:

A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically decide
which changes to keep.

To resolve it:

1. Open the conflicted file.
2. Identify the conflict markers.
3. Keep the required changes.
4. Remove the conflict markers.
5. git add <file>
6. git commit
7. Check git status.
